   "id": 1,
   "title": "Fire safety and the law",
   "minutes": 8,
   "html": "<p>Fire safety is not just the job of a manager or a fire warden. The law puts duties on the organisation <em>and</em> on every person who works there. Knowing your part is the first step.</p>\n\n<h3>The main law</h3>\n<p>In England and Wales, workplace fire safety is governed by the <strong>Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005</strong>, usually called the Fire Safety Order. It applies to almost every non-domestic building, plus the common parts of blocks of flats and houses in multiple occupation. Scotland and Northern Ireland have their own laws that work in a very similar way.</p>\n\n<h3>The responsible person</h3>\n<p>The Order places the main duties on the <strong>responsible person</strong>. In a workplace this is usually the employer; elsewhere it can be the owner, landlord or whoever controls the premises. Their duties include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>carrying out a <strong>fire risk assessment</strong> and keeping it under review;</li>\n<li>putting fire precautions in place, such as alarms, escape routes, emergency lighting and extinguishers;</li>\n<li>planning for emergencies and nominating <strong>competent persons</strong> (often called fire wardens or marshals) to help evacuate people;</li>\n<li>giving staff information and <strong>fire safety training</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>When training must happen</h3>\n<p>Article 21 of the Order says staff must receive adequate training <strong>when they start</strong> and again when they are exposed to <strong>new or increased risks</strong>, for example a new role, new equipment or a new way of working. It must be repeated periodically, and Home Office guidance treats <strong>at least once a year</strong> as recognised practice. Higher-risk premises such as care homes usually need more.</p>\n\n<h3>Your duties as an employee</h3>\n<p>Article 23 puts duties on employees too. You must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>take reasonable care for your own safety and the safety of others affected by what you do;</li>\n<li>cooperate with your employer so they can meet their fire safety duties;</li>\n<li>tell your employer about any situation you believe is a serious and immediate danger, or any shortcoming in the fire safety arrangements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In practice that means following procedures, never tampering with fire equipment, keeping routes clear and reporting problems instead of walking past them.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Key point:</strong> the employer leads, but everyone has a legal duty. Reporting a wedged fire door or a blocked exit is part of your job.</div>",
